I have recently downloaded and started using the CHET fonts
recently posted here.  I have a few comments about their use.

The game needs some improvements in the visual display area to make
these fonts a Godsend.  These fonts will replace the characters used
by the game with more graphical images on the screen.  This works
well except for the punctuation characters.  The " used by amulets,
the + used by spellbooks, the ? used by scrolls, etc, etc, etc.
While most of the dungeon characters can be mapped in the NETHACK.CNF
file by using GRAPHICS =, too many of the item characters are HARD
CODED to the ascii values.  If these items were definable (either
via the same or a similar method), then I could still read the text
and use the font sets too.  ?, +, ", !, are all used in text messages
as punctuation, and should remain that way.  The characters used for
scrolls, spellbooks, amulets, potions, rock, etc  should be user
definable (and should default to their current ASCII values.

I find the Gothic test amusing.  I have gotten some great comments
on it by some random passer's by while playing.   BUT  (and this is
a BIG BUT), I am having trouble distinguishing a couple of the upper
case letters from the lower case ones.  Particularly D, d, and K, k.

You see there was this game where I was looting this bookstore, and
found this blessed scroll of genocide, a number of scrolls of identify,
and a scroll of teleportation (or two).  So I loaded up, teleported
out, and headed for the stairs.  On my way a ran into the Kops.

So, easy way out? Genocide them.  Oops I genocided all kobolds instead
of all Kops.  How revolting!  Of course since I was only on the
second level, I died.   AAAAAAAAAAAARRRRRRRRRRRRRGGGGGGGGGGGGHHHHHHHHHH!

Die and learn I guess, but if there was a bigger difference in
the character on the screen, I wouldn't have screwed it up!

So for the DEV team:  I know, its a lot of work tracking down all those
hard coded character references in the source code, but in the end it
will definately be worth it.

Also, it would be nice if we could also specify a color along with the
character (for those of us with color displays).  I would like to
make sure that certain dungeon features stand out more than they
do now on my display, and Grey just doesn't cut it.  I hate the color
used for fountains (Why can't they be light blue instead of dark
blue?).  And I want my altars to be bright RED.  And I want my ....
well, you get the idea.

Now, if we can do the same for Monsters, it's going to be one heck
of a FONT file on a character device!


Oh, and by the way.  I still haven't gotten any real information
about whether xnethack was ever done for a version 3.x of NetHack.
I have the version for 2.something, but not the Nethack version 2
source code.  Maybe some of those characters can be used int the
font file.  They had a great characterization for dogs and cats
and some of the other more common monsters.

For the DEV team:  Are you interested in trying to put the
xnethack support into the released copies?  It would then work
with X windows on a number of different ports (not just UNIX).

I have the descriptions of what was done for NetHack 2.x.

